211
00:07:38.829 --> 00:07:41.083
I'm much more interested in the sports

212
00:07:41.083 --> 00:07:43.103
that we're gonna cover in a few moments.

213
00:07:43.103 --> 00:07:44.061
The Olympics being

214
00:07:44.061 --> 00:07:47.978
at the top of that sort of pyramid of interest.

215
00:07:50.696 --> 00:07:55.011
You know, but professional sports is not my bailiwick.

216
00:07:55.011 --> 00:07:56.344
Be that as it may,

217
00:07:56.344 --> 00:07:59.367
what I love about sports is I bet you could tell me

218
00:07:59.367 --> 00:08:01.575
who the two best players on the Rams are

219
00:08:01.575 --> 00:08:02.512
just from watching a game.

220
00:08:02.512 --> 00:08:03.475
You haven't seen them play all year,

221
00:08:03.475 --> 00:08:04.655
you don't know anything about the team,

222
00:08:04.655 --> 00:08:06.113
and you probably could tell me who they are.

223
00:08:06.113 --> 00:08:07.196
No, we should move on.

224
00:08:07.196 --> 00:08:08.029
Oh.

225
00:08:08.029 --> 00:08:09.487
(Rich laughs)

226
00:08:09.487 --> 00:08:10.820
I enjoyed the commercials though.

227
00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.098
It's Cooper Kupp and Aaron Donald.

228
00:08:14.098 --> 00:08:14.931
Larry David won-

229
00:08:14.931 --> 00:08:16.028
Well, Kupp scored two touchdowns.

230
00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:17.487
He did and Aaron Donald-

231
00:08:17.487 --> 00:08:19.868
The game got very exciting in the last five minutes.

232
00:08:19.868 --> 00:08:21.756
Although it was a bit of a slog before that.

233
00:08:21.756 --> 00:08:24.714
A bit of a slog, a bit of a slog.

234
00:08:24.714 --> 00:08:26.672
Anyway, enough of that.

235
00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.406
Let's turn to the topics that we wanna get into today.

236
00:08:30.406 --> 00:08:31.239
Let's do it.

237
00:08:31.239 --> 00:08:33.542
Specifically the Winter Olympics.

238
00:08:33.542 --> 00:08:35.223
But I wanna talk about the Olympics

239
00:08:35.223 --> 00:08:39.989
through this kind of interesting lens of declining interest,

240
00:08:39.989 --> 00:08:40.822
right?

241
00:08:40.822 --> 00:08:42.467
Have you been watching the Winter Olympics?

242
00:08:42.467 --> 00:08:43.550
Like, what is your perspective

243
00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.365
on how this has been transpiring

244
00:08:45.365 --> 00:08:48.059
in terms of the media attention

245
00:08:48.059 --> 00:08:50.743
and just general, you know, public engagement

246
00:08:50.743 --> 00:08:51.951
with what's happening?

247
00:08:51.951 --> 00:08:54.451
I have not been watching it.

248
00:08:55.909 --> 00:08:57.667
But I have been reading stories on it

249
00:08:57.667 --> 00:08:59.625
because some colleagues of mine

250
00:08:59.625 --> 00:09:01.642
in The New York Times Sports are over there,

251
00:09:01.642 --> 00:09:04.351
and they do a fantastic job every Olympics,

252
00:09:04.351 --> 00:09:05.559
and they're doing a great job again.

253
00:09:05.559 --> 00:09:08.832
And so, I kind of have been cluing into the main stories

254
00:09:08.832 --> 00:09:10.415
and the winners.

255
00:09:10.415 --> 00:09:13.832
And for me it's because it's, you know...

256
00:09:16.054 --> 00:09:19.957
It's a tough one just because of my life right now

257
00:09:19.957 --> 00:09:21.165
with a little one.

258
00:09:21.165 --> 00:09:24.115
Like, I can't really make time to like sit down

259
00:09:24.115 --> 00:09:26.066
and absorb these longer events

260
00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:28.735
and all these heats and all that,

261
00:09:28.735 --> 00:09:30.309
you know, after the kid goes down

262
00:09:30.309 --> 00:09:31.642
because I'm up at like 5:00.

263
00:09:31.642 --> 00:09:33.475
So, there's really no time for it.

264
00:09:33.475 --> 00:09:36.010
And so that's really the only reason it's,

265
00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.454
and then in general,

266
00:09:37.454 --> 00:09:39.714
I kind of don't tune into the Winter Olympics

267
00:09:39.714 --> 00:09:40.887
in a regular basis anyway.

268
00:09:40.887 --> 00:09:42.898
Like, there are some times I do there.

269
00:09:42.898 --> 00:09:44.925
There are some years I do some.

270
00:09:44.925 --> 00:09:46.246
Some, it's not years.

271
00:09:46.246 --> 00:09:48.410
Some, what's the word for four years?

272
00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.868
I don't know.

273
00:09:49.868 --> 00:09:51.326
Quad annual.

274
00:09:51.326 --> 00:09:52.909
Quadrennial.

275
00:09:52.909 --> 00:09:55.344
Some quadrennials I do tune in,

276
00:09:55.344 --> 00:09:57.497
but this quadrennial I have not.

277
00:09:57.497 --> 00:09:58.330
Yeah, it's interesting.

278
00:09:58.330 --> 00:09:59.413
I mean, I think, you know,

279
00:09:59.413 --> 00:10:00.621
certainly there's some amazing stories,

280
00:10:00.621 --> 00:10:04.489
and NBC is covering it in the way that they always do,

281
00:10:04.489 --> 00:10:06.677
and now they have Peacock so you can stream it,

282
00:10:06.677 --> 00:10:08.737
which wasn't the case in the prior Olympics.

283
00:10:08.737 --> 00:10:10.031
And you would think with that,

284
00:10:10.031 --> 00:10:13.377
there would be this sort of audience capture.

285
00:10:13.377 --> 00:10:16.136
But my sense is that that's not the case,

286
00:10:16.136 --> 00:10:18.433
and it's left me kind of reflecting on

287
00:10:18.433 --> 00:10:22.428
what the Olympics mean personally and at large.

288
00:10:22.428 --> 00:10:23.886
And when I think back, you know,

289
00:10:23.886 --> 00:10:25.219
over the course of my own life,

290
00:10:25.219 --> 00:10:29.234
the Olympics has been huge as an inspiration

291
00:10:29.234 --> 00:10:32.434
and as a motivational force in many decisions

292
00:10:32.434 --> 00:10:33.696
that I made in my life.

293
00:10:33.696 --> 00:10:37.024
Like, I remember being a kid, I'm older than you,

294
00:10:37.024 --> 00:10:39.212
and just being obsessed with the Olympics.

295
00:10:39.212 --> 00:10:42.131
And this was a time when Sports Illustrated really mattered,

296
00:10:42.131 --> 00:10:44.829
and you would wait for that latest edition

297
00:10:44.829 --> 00:10:45.912
to come in the mail,

298
00:10:45.912 --> 00:10:48.184
and there would always be an incredible photograph

299
00:10:48.184 --> 00:10:49.017
on the cover.

300
00:10:49.017 --> 00:10:51.944
They did a such a wonderful job of covering the Olympics.

301
00:10:51.944 --> 00:10:54.652
And, you know, within that

302
00:10:54.652 --> 00:10:56.416
in the context of the Winter Olympics,

303
00:10:56.416 --> 00:11:00.064
like I have vivid memories of the Miracle on Ice,

304
00:11:00.064 --> 00:11:03.590
the 1980 US hockey victory that became this movie,

305
00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:04.983
which is actually a really good movie.

306
00:11:04.983 --> 00:11:06.191
Have you seen "Miracle?"

307
00:11:06.191 --> 00:11:07.399
I have seen "Miracle."

308
00:11:07.399 --> 00:11:08.857
It's been a while.
"Miracle" holds up.

309
00:11:08.857 --> 00:11:09.875
But I mean, I remember,

310
00:11:09.875 --> 00:11:12.076
I mean, I'm old enough to have been kind

311
00:11:12.076 --> 00:11:14.314
of a semi-conscious sports fan in 1980.

312
00:11:14.314 --> 00:11:16.989
That was the most remarkable team.

313
00:11:16.989 --> 00:11:20.669
That was probably, if you had to pick the Olympics

314
00:11:20.669 --> 00:11:23.523
that really turned me onto it, that was the Olympics,

315
00:11:23.523 --> 00:11:25.047
the 1980 Winter Olympics.
Yeah, it was incredible.

316
00:11:25.047 --> 00:11:27.408
So, my first favorite Olympics was Winter Olympics.

317
00:11:27.408 --> 00:11:29.306
Right, so we had the Miracle on Ice

318
00:11:29.306 --> 00:11:31.476
where literally the whole world stopped,

319
00:11:31.476 --> 00:11:35.092
and everybody was tuned in to what was happening.

320
00:11:35.092 --> 00:11:38.949
And it was a, you know, incredible monoculture moment

321
00:11:38.949 --> 00:11:41.715
that now in today's fractured, you know,

322
00:11:41.715 --> 00:11:44.382
kind of media ecosystem is rare.

323
00:11:45.637 --> 00:11:47.298
Those moments are few and far between.

324
00:11:47.298 --> 00:11:48.340
I mean, the Super Bowl is one

325
00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:49.968
of the few remaining things like that

326
00:11:49.968 --> 00:11:52.126
and the occasional Marvel movie.

327
00:11:52.126 --> 00:11:56.709
But beyond that, you're hard pressed to think of things

328
00:11:57.587 --> 00:12:00.268
that happen in sports or in culture in general

329
00:12:00.268 --> 00:12:03.469
that make everybody pay attention at the same time.

330
00:12:03.469 --> 00:12:04.552
It just doesn't exist.

331
00:12:04.552 --> 00:12:06.870
The Miracle on Ice was like an incredible moment.

332
00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.802
And I remember, you know, Mike Eruzione and Jim Craig

333
00:12:09.802 --> 00:12:11.186
and Herb Brooks, the coach,

334
00:12:11.186 --> 00:12:13.232
and all the intrigue around that.

335
00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:15.843
And I think the movie, if you haven't seen "Miracle,"

336
00:12:15.843 --> 00:12:18.864
it does a really good job of capturing that,

337
00:12:18.864 --> 00:12:20.317
and it does hold up.

338
00:12:20.317 --> 00:12:22.933
"The Rewatchables," which is a Ringer podcast

339
00:12:22.933 --> 00:12:24.250
where they go over old movies,

340
00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.450
they did a recent episode on "Miracle,"

341
00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.778
which if you're into that kind of thing was super fun

342
00:12:28.778 --> 00:12:29.611
to listen to.

343
00:12:29.611 --> 00:12:30.944
I listened to that when I was in Hawaii.

344
00:12:30.944 --> 00:12:31.777
Oh, that's cool.

345
00:12:31.777 --> 00:12:33.235
And then beyond that, like my personal,

346
00:12:33.235 --> 00:12:36.460
one of my favorite athletes of all time

347
00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.025
from that same Olympiad was Eric Heiden

348
00:12:39.025 --> 00:12:41.209
who won five golds in speed skating,

349
00:12:41.209 --> 00:12:44.500
set four Olympic records, one world record,

350
00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:48.851
gathered more medals personally than any other country

351
00:12:48.851 --> 00:12:49.934
in that Olympiad,

352
00:12:49.934 --> 00:12:52.441
other than the Soviet Union and East Germany.

353
00:12:52.441 --> 00:12:55.076
And then he becomes this pro cyclist for 7/11,

354
00:12:55.076 --> 00:12:57.183
and he was one of the first crossover athletes.

355
00:12:57.183 --> 00:12:59.524
It's like, I remember that cover of Sports Illustrated

356
00:12:59.524 --> 00:13:01.951
when he was in the all gold like body suit,

357
00:13:01.951 --> 00:13:03.747
and his quads were just enormous.

358
00:13:03.747 --> 00:13:07.841
And I just thought that was the most unbelievable,

359
00:13:07.841 --> 00:13:09.174
coolest thing I'd ever seen

360
00:13:09.174 --> 00:13:10.996
'cause I didn't know anything about speed skating

361
00:13:10.996 --> 00:13:11.954
before that.

362
00:13:11.954 --> 00:13:12.912
Yeah.

363
00:13:12.912 --> 00:13:13.745
The-

364
00:13:13.745 --> 00:13:15.626
And speed skating is like made for television.

365
00:13:15.626 --> 00:13:16.817
It is such a cool-
It's pretty cool.

366
00:13:16.817 --> 00:13:17.808
It is such a cool thing to watch.

367
00:13:17.808 --> 00:13:18.641
Yeah, it's very cool.

368
00:13:18.641 --> 00:13:19.474
Yeah, yeah.

369
00:13:19.474 --> 00:13:21.144
Just the way in which the Olympics,

370
00:13:21.144 --> 00:13:23.970
even with sports that are so kind of askance

371
00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.320
of mainstream interest could galvanize the world

372
00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.028
for a given moment in time,

373
00:13:29.028 --> 00:13:32.630
it just doesn't feel like that's the case right now.

374
00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.000
And there's a lot of reasons for that,

375
00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.333
which I want to get into.

376
00:13:36.333 --> 00:13:39.322
Not the least of which is, you know, doping scandals,

377
00:13:39.322 --> 00:13:43.655
IOC corruption, and just our fractured media culture

378
00:13:45.645 --> 00:13:47.917
where there's too many choices,

379
00:13:47.917 --> 00:13:50.510
and it's very difficult to drive everybody's attention

380
00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:51.468
into one thing.

381
00:13:51.468 --> 00:13:53.363
I mean, when the '80 Olympics was going on,

382
00:13:53.363 --> 00:13:54.449
there was like four TV channels

383
00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:56.002
and that was basically it.

384
00:13:56.002 --> 00:13:56.835
Right.

385
00:13:56.835 --> 00:13:58.541
And you had to watch when it was happening,

386
00:13:58.541 --> 00:14:02.106
you couldn't tape it or, you know, time delay it.

387
00:14:02.106 --> 00:14:03.886
And there was no Twitter or internet-

388
00:14:03.886 --> 00:14:05.219
Who they would delay it for you.

389
00:14:05.219 --> 00:14:06.302
They would delay it for you

390
00:14:06.302 --> 00:14:07.510
and funnel it to your prime time.

391
00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:10.163
It was very easy to avoid knowing what had happened.

392
00:14:10.163 --> 00:14:13.291
It didn't matter because the newspapers wouldn't report it

393
00:14:13.291 --> 00:14:14.265
'til the following day.

394
00:14:14.265 --> 00:14:16.413
So, it didn't matter if it had happened already.

395
00:14:16.413 --> 00:14:17.371
There was like,

396
00:14:17.371 --> 00:14:21.871
unless you're like paying attention to the radio news,

397
00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.496
you weren't gonna get that headline until the following day.

398
00:14:26.496 --> 00:14:30.369
So, they could delay it 'til dinnertime or after.

399
00:14:30.369 --> 00:14:32.667
And when you think about why, you know,

400
00:14:32.667 --> 00:14:36.250
sort of the sheen has faded off the Olympic brand

401
00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:37.985
and why it's this brand

402
00:14:37.985 --> 00:14:42.046
that I think you can make a fair argument is in decline

403
00:14:42.046 --> 00:14:44.763
is the dissonance between what it stands for,

404
00:14:44.763 --> 00:14:48.870
which is like principles and values and ethics,

405
00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:52.883
has been tainted by so much controversy and corruption,

406
00:14:52.883 --> 00:14:55.668
and this dissonance between what the Olympics represents

407
00:14:55.668 --> 00:14:58.600
and what it actually is in reality,

408
00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:00.683
there's a huge gap there.

409
00:15:01.558 --> 00:15:03.701
And of course, then that's even further handicapped

410
00:15:03.701 --> 00:15:06.477
by our media dispersed culture.

411
00:15:06.477 --> 00:15:07.685
Media dispersed meaning?

412
00:15:07.685 --> 00:15:09.448
Meaning like there's just too many choices.

413
00:15:09.448 --> 00:15:10.781
Right.

414
00:15:10.781 --> 00:15:13.280
You miss the old days when the brainwashing came right

415
00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.778
to your living room.

416
00:15:14.778 --> 00:15:16.861
And I welcomed it gladly.

417
00:15:16.861 --> 00:15:19.449
I let the propaganda wash right over me.

418
00:15:19.449 --> 00:15:20.282
Yeah.

419
00:15:20.282 --> 00:15:22.714
As I draped myself in the American flag.

420
00:15:22.714 --> 00:15:24.179
And then at like 2:00 in the morning,

421
00:15:24.179 --> 00:15:25.825
the American flag came on your television,

422
00:15:25.825 --> 00:15:29.099
then it went to the white snow, whatever that-

423
00:15:29.099 --> 00:15:29.932
Yeah, exactly.

424
00:15:29.932 --> 00:15:30.765
Yeah, the static.

425
00:15:30.765 --> 00:15:31.598
Exactly.

426
00:15:31.598 --> 00:15:32.681
Boy, those were great days.

427
00:15:32.681 --> 00:15:34.389
How I wish for that.

428
00:15:34.389 --> 00:15:35.972
But I think that played a big part

429
00:15:35.972 --> 00:15:37.462
in me wanting to be a swimmer

430
00:15:37.462 --> 00:15:40.173
and just loving everything about the Olympics

431
00:15:40.173 --> 00:15:41.381
and the Olympic movement,

432
00:15:41.381 --> 00:15:44.103
and then being able to train with other Olympic athletes.

433
00:15:44.103 --> 00:15:46.364
It's played such a huge part in my life,

434
00:15:46.364 --> 00:15:49.613
which is why, you know, it's so dispiriting

435
00:15:49.613 --> 00:15:53.057
to me personally to see this thing kind of falling apart

436
00:15:53.057 --> 00:15:54.515
before our very eyes.

437
00:15:54.515 --> 00:15:57.307
And so, to kind of further get into, you know,

438
00:15:57.307 --> 00:15:59.265
the hows and whys of that,

439
00:15:59.265 --> 00:16:03.159
I wanted to draw attention to a newsletter

440
00:16:03.159 --> 00:16:05.261
that David Epstein puts out.

441
00:16:05.261 --> 00:16:08.029
David being a friend of the podcast.

442
00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:10.612
He was on in 2016, episode 466.

443
00:16:12.061 --> 00:16:15.375
He's the author of some great books, "Range,"

444
00:16:15.375 --> 00:16:16.379
"The Sports Gene,"

445
00:16:16.379 --> 00:16:20.716
and he has this newsletter called Range Widely.

446
00:16:20.716 --> 00:16:22.966
And he posted this article-

447
00:16:24.549 --> 00:16:25.382
Great article.

448
00:16:25.382 --> 00:16:27.562
Called "The Olympics Have an Integrity Problem.

449
00:16:27.562 --> 00:16:28.645
"Here's How to Fix It,"

450
00:16:28.645 --> 00:16:31.470
wherein he interviews this guy, Steve Mesler,

451
00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.866
who's a three time Olympian

452
00:16:32.866 --> 00:16:35.365
who had won a gold medal in bobsled

453
00:16:35.365 --> 00:16:38.643
and is now a board member of the United States Olympic

454
00:16:38.643 --> 00:16:40.703
and Paralympic Committee.

455
00:16:40.703 --> 00:16:42.882
And what was so interesting about this interview is

456
00:16:42.882 --> 00:16:44.340
how frank this guy was

457
00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.840
because he was sort of talking outta school

458
00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:50.684
in terms of what he thinks is going wrong as a current,

459
00:16:50.684 --> 00:16:55.017
you know, sitting board member of this organization.

460
00:16:56.365 --> 00:16:57.865
Being the USOPC.

461
00:16:59.164 --> 00:17:00.122
Right, and, you know,

462
00:17:00.122 --> 00:17:01.528
we could point to a number of factors.

463
00:17:01.528 --> 00:17:02.541
I mean, the fact that in,

464
00:17:02.541 --> 00:17:06.555
you know, this came up in my podcast with Brian Fogel,

465
00:17:06.555 --> 00:17:08.706
the documentarian behind "Icarus"

466
00:17:08.706 --> 00:17:12.398
who explored Russian state sponsored doping.

467
00:17:12.398 --> 00:17:16.087
So in 2019, Russia gets banned from the Olympics.

468
00:17:16.087 --> 00:17:18.140
But as we all know, they're like still competing

469
00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.223
as the Russian Olympic Committee, which is just,

470
00:17:20.223 --> 00:17:22.425
and we all just kind of like go, "Okay, oh well, I guess."

471
00:17:22.425 --> 00:17:23.405
They were banned before Tokyo.

472
00:17:23.405 --> 00:17:24.738
I mean, I think my...

473
00:17:24.738 --> 00:17:26.807
I think, so when it first happened,

474
00:17:26.807 --> 00:17:30.557
and they had the ROC got to compete in Tokyo,

475
00:17:32.679 --> 00:17:35.656
my sympathies were a little bit with the Russian athlete

476
00:17:35.656 --> 00:17:37.071
that may be clean.

477
00:17:37.071 --> 00:17:39.404
Like, you know, maybe, so I-

478
00:17:40.452 --> 00:17:42.654
Well, if the doping is state sponsored,

479
00:17:42.654 --> 00:17:45.881
on some level like that denigrates the culpability

480
00:17:45.881 --> 00:17:47.293
of the individual athlete.

481
00:17:47.293 --> 00:17:49.985
So, this is not about pointing fingers at athletes.

482
00:17:49.985 --> 00:17:51.559
This is about systemic problems

483
00:17:51.559 --> 00:17:53.792
and power politics that are at play.

484
00:17:53.792 --> 00:17:54.625
Right.

485
00:17:54.625 --> 00:17:56.886
And so, but the bottom line is if a country continues

486
00:17:56.886 --> 00:17:59.043
to violate, then the athletes may get penalized,

487
00:17:59.043 --> 00:18:00.628
but that's just part and parcel of it.

488
00:18:00.628 --> 00:18:01.961
So, you can't like, there might,

489
00:18:01.961 --> 00:18:05.310
even if you're an athlete that is under that flag

490
00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:10.081
and the, you can't trust that country to play a fair game,

491
00:18:10.081 --> 00:18:12.831
then I do understand the penalty,

492
00:18:14.539 --> 00:18:16.977
but then you gotta actually administer the penalty.

493
00:18:16.977 --> 00:18:20.273
You can't like say it's a penalty and then just like-

494
00:18:20.273 --> 00:18:22.697
Find some technicality that allows it to.

495
00:18:22.697 --> 00:18:23.859
They did the same thing.

496
00:18:23.859 --> 00:18:26.106
Putin was supposedly banned,

497
00:18:26.106 --> 00:18:29.450
but then there he is at the opening ceremonies in Beijing

498
00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.911
'cause they created some kind of corollary rule

499
00:18:31.911 --> 00:18:34.159
that allowed heads of state to attend

500
00:18:34.159 --> 00:18:36.867
despite the Russian ban.

501
00:18:36.867 --> 00:18:39.066
He was invited by the head of China.

502
00:18:39.066 --> 00:18:41.438
Yeah, I mean, the duplicity here is unbelievable.

503
00:18:41.438 --> 00:18:46.234
And, you know, we have opulent demands by the IOC members,

504
00:18:46.234 --> 00:18:48.616
all the corruption that goes on behind the scenes

505
00:18:48.616 --> 00:18:50.449
with the bidding.

506
00:18:50.449 --> 00:18:53.006
How about the Uyghur athlete lighting the flame?

507
00:18:53.006 --> 00:18:53.839
Yeah.

508
00:18:53.839 --> 00:18:54.672
Which is sort of a,

509
00:18:54.672 --> 00:18:58.900
sort of a political whitewashing of, you know,

510
00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:01.379
a pretty terrible situation.

511
00:19:01.379 --> 00:19:02.440
Of labor camp.

512
00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:03.398
Of abuse.

513
00:19:03.398 --> 00:19:04.356
Within China.

514
00:19:04.356 --> 00:19:05.189
Yeah, within China.

515
00:19:05.189 --> 00:19:08.636
And now I guess future Olympiads are being awarded

516
00:19:08.636 --> 00:19:09.469
without bidding

517
00:19:09.469 --> 00:19:11.536
because, as David points out in this newsletter,

518
00:19:11.536 --> 00:19:13.300
there's a decline in cities bidding for this.

519
00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:16.245
Like, there isn't the demand that there once was.

520
00:19:16.245 --> 00:19:18.372
Like, it used to be this huge prestigious thing

521
00:19:18.372 --> 00:19:19.716
to land an Olympiad,

522
00:19:19.716 --> 00:19:23.639
and now very few cities are even interested in hosting.

523
00:19:23.639 --> 00:19:25.222
Because it costs.

524
00:19:26.597 --> 00:19:29.468
So, people, you'd have to build arenas, you'd have to,

525
00:19:29.468 --> 00:19:30.676
it costs so much to put it on,

526
00:19:30.676 --> 00:19:32.371
but yet you'd still, they would still bid,

527
00:19:32.371 --> 00:19:35.329
they would still make these incredible,

528
00:19:35.329 --> 00:19:38.539
you know, presentations and bid, you know,

529
00:19:38.539 --> 00:19:40.828
I don't know if it got to $100 million,

530
00:19:40.828 --> 00:19:41.911
but I believe it did.

531
00:19:41.911 --> 00:19:43.244
I think it's in excess of that.

532
00:19:43.244 --> 00:19:46.036
Didn't LA decide to defer an Olympiad

533
00:19:46.036 --> 00:19:50.263
in exchange for like $180 million bonus or whatever?

534
00:19:50.263 --> 00:19:52.765
Like, I think David points out or,

535
00:19:52.765 --> 00:19:53.848
is it David or Steve?

536
00:19:53.848 --> 00:19:56.372
Someone points out what happened.

537
00:19:56.372 --> 00:19:59.822
Paris and LA both went for 2024,

538
00:19:59.822 --> 00:20:02.780
and they both won basically.

539
00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:03.829
The Olympic committee said,

540
00:20:03.829 --> 00:20:04.662
"We'll give it to Paris,

541
00:20:04.662 --> 00:20:08.511
"and we'll give you, LA, $100+ million if you'll take 2028."

542
00:20:08.511 --> 00:20:11.780
And then they awarded Brisbane in 2032 without any bids

543
00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:13.968
because they didn't want to be embarrassed

544
00:20:13.968 --> 00:20:16.199
by only having one bid or no bids.

545
00:20:16.199 --> 00:20:18.778
And so maybe now it's like, you're almost recruiting.

546
00:20:18.778 --> 00:20:21.364
It's almost, they're actually having to pay.

547
00:20:21.364 --> 00:20:22.822
There's a desperation with that.

548
00:20:22.822 --> 00:20:25.530
And why Brisbane, Adam?

549
00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.113
Because it's awesome.

550
00:20:27.113 --> 00:20:28.274
Well, maybe.

551
00:20:28.274 --> 00:20:29.732
It is awesome, yeah.

552
00:20:29.732 --> 00:20:32.061
I can see it being a cool place to host the Olympics.

553
00:20:32.061 --> 00:20:34.368
But let's go behind the scenes a little bit.

554
00:20:34.368 --> 00:20:35.201
The gold coast.

555
00:20:35.201 --> 00:20:37.463
And understand, first of all,

556
00:20:37.463 --> 00:20:42.347
the kind of political co-mingling between WADA and the IOC

557
00:20:42.347 --> 00:20:43.430
that's highly problematic.

558
00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:44.388
Oh, that's right, yes.

559
00:20:44.388 --> 00:20:47.143
Half of WADA seats are occupied by IOC members,

560
00:20:47.143 --> 00:20:51.282
which is insane because basically, you know,

561
00:20:51.282 --> 00:20:53.740
one of these organizations is a police force

562
00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:55.847
and the other is a promotional force,

563
00:20:55.847 --> 00:20:58.187
and these two things should not intersect.

564
00:20:58.187 --> 00:21:01.145
They should be checks and balances on each other,

565
00:21:01.145 --> 00:21:03.510
and yet they're completely co-mingled,

566
00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.154
so much so that John Coates,

567
00:21:05.154 --> 00:21:08.840
who's president of the Australian Olympic Committee

568
00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:12.840
and who is on the IOC board, he's VP of the IOC,

569
00:21:13.825 --> 00:21:17.851
is also president of the Court of Arbitration for Sport.

570
00:21:17.851 --> 00:21:20.642
So, he's overseeing jurisprudence at the same time.

571
00:21:20.642 --> 00:21:21.475
Insane.

572
00:21:21.475 --> 00:21:22.473
I mean, talk about, it's like, you know,

573
00:21:22.473 --> 00:21:24.821
in government you have these different houses

574
00:21:24.821 --> 00:21:26.188
with different responsibilities.

575
00:21:26.188 --> 00:21:29.582
The judiciary and the legislature should not be co-mingled,

576
00:21:29.582 --> 00:21:30.415
right?

577
00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:32.998
And he's also the final,

578
00:21:32.998 --> 00:21:35.104
which, well, the Court of Arbitration for Sport is

579
00:21:35.104 --> 00:21:37.764
the final arbiter of WADA decisions.

580
00:21:37.764 --> 00:21:40.750
And he's leader of the Brisbane bid.

581
00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:41.708
I guess they won.

582
00:21:41.708 --> 00:21:42.666
So, you tell me, you know,

583
00:21:42.666 --> 00:21:44.499
how that came to pass, right?

584
00:21:44.499 --> 00:21:45.332
They won.

585
00:21:45.332 --> 00:21:47.806
So, here we have this unbelievably corrupt

586
00:21:47.806 --> 00:21:52.806
and broken system of bribes and political favoritism

587
00:21:52.962 --> 00:21:56.528
and doping scandals and failure to adhere

588
00:21:56.528 --> 00:21:59.253
to, you know, rules that I thought we agreed upon.

589
00:21:59.253 --> 00:22:00.586
And the writer, like you said,

590
00:22:00.586 --> 00:22:05.464
that these IOC members' opulent demands basically amount to,

591
00:22:05.464 --> 00:22:07.638
David compares 'em to a concert writer

592
00:22:07.638 --> 00:22:09.221
from like a diva star.

593
00:22:09.221 --> 00:22:10.660
And like they're demanding-

594
00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.243
They wanna be wined and dined.

595
00:22:12.243 --> 00:22:13.076
Wined and dined.

596
00:22:13.076 --> 00:22:14.159
They want their own traffic lanes.

597
00:22:14.159 --> 00:22:16.652
They want like, I mean, it's crazy the demands they make

598
00:22:16.652 --> 00:22:21.091
and, you know, there's been incredible corruption

599
00:22:21.091 --> 00:22:24.091
in how IOC members are being bribed.

600
00:22:25.616 --> 00:22:28.014
There's accusations of being bribed with money

601
00:22:28.014 --> 00:22:30.141
and prostitutes in that story.

602
00:22:30.141 --> 00:22:30.974
It's a crazy story.

603
00:22:30.974 --> 00:22:31.807
I know.

604
00:22:31.807 --> 00:22:34.507
So, when you think of the Olympics as a brand, right,

605
00:22:34.507 --> 00:22:37.760
what an incredible brand because of what it stands for.

606
00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.335
But if you denigrate, like, if you,

607
00:22:40.335 --> 00:22:44.196
if the foundation of that brand is eroded and the principles

608
00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:48.279
and values upon which it stands no longer become,

609
00:22:50.272 --> 00:22:53.466
are no longer kind of like practiced in reality,

610
00:22:53.466 --> 00:22:55.133
then what does that brand stand for?

611
00:22:55.133 --> 00:22:58.688
And I think that's really at the root of the denigration

612
00:22:58.688 --> 00:23:00.146
of the Olympics,

613
00:23:00.146 --> 00:23:03.320
and I think in turn, people's interest in it.

614
00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:07.903
Like, there's a cynicism now and kind of less sanguine,

615
00:23:09.679 --> 00:23:11.483
you know, relationship that we all have

616
00:23:11.483 --> 00:23:14.731
because we've been clued into some of these things

617
00:23:14.731 --> 00:23:16.311
that are going on behind the scenes.

618
00:23:16.311 --> 00:23:17.887
And, you know, I don't know if it's worse now

619
00:23:17.887 --> 00:23:18.970
than it always has been,

620
00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:20.963
or maybe we're just pulling the covers better

621
00:23:20.963 --> 00:23:24.092
than we ever have before, but it's a problem.

622
00:23:24.092 --> 00:23:26.357
You know, I feel like the Olympic brand is sort of

623
00:23:26.357 --> 00:23:27.190
in free fall,

624
00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:30.362
and they're trying to do damage control

625
00:23:30.362 --> 00:23:34.756
at a time right now while the games are going on

626
00:23:34.756 --> 00:23:38.177
where it should be all about celebrating the athletes

627
00:23:38.177 --> 00:23:41.802
and the spirit of what this is kind of about.

628
00:23:41.802 --> 00:23:42.635
I agree with you.

629
00:23:42.635 --> 00:23:46.403
I think that we're at a time in human history

630
00:23:46.403 --> 00:23:51.154
where this disparity between the wealthy and the poor is

631
00:23:51.154 --> 00:23:53.452
at the biggest I think of our lifetimes

632
00:23:53.452 --> 00:23:55.806
and maybe the biggest in history,

633
00:23:55.806 --> 00:24:00.361
and that kind of greed I think does infect these brands.

634
00:24:00.361 --> 00:24:02.508
And I think it's twofold.

635
00:24:02.508 --> 00:24:04.979
I think probably there was always a certain amount

636
00:24:04.979 --> 00:24:08.644
of corruption and racism and cynicism built

637
00:24:08.644 --> 00:24:11.658
into these structures that we all revered.

638
00:24:11.658 --> 00:24:15.752
But I also think that there was more honor.

639
00:24:15.752 --> 00:24:19.983
I do think there was more honor, organizationally.

640
00:24:19.983 --> 00:24:22.651
I think there was more honor in terms of how people,

641
00:24:22.651 --> 00:24:26.900
you know, being fair and having certain values,

642
00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:28.858
and honoring the brand itself.

643
00:24:28.858 --> 00:24:32.602
I mean, you could look at any sport,

644
00:24:32.602 --> 00:24:34.699
like everything's up for sale.

645
00:24:34.699 --> 00:24:35.903
Corporate sponsorship left and right.

646
00:24:35.903 --> 00:24:38.210
And so, when you get into that place,

647
00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:42.527
the money becomes the God and the values are secondary.

648
00:24:42.527 --> 00:24:44.036
Now maybe money was always that way.

649
00:24:44.036 --> 00:24:47.254
But I do think that it has eroded a bit more

650
00:24:47.254 --> 00:24:49.654
in recent years, in recent decades anyway.

651
00:24:49.654 --> 00:24:50.487
At the same time,

652
00:24:50.487 --> 00:24:53.803
I think that everything's up for review right now,

653
00:24:53.803 --> 00:24:58.053
so it's not surprising to see the Olympics get the,

654
00:24:59.102 --> 00:25:01.417
you know, just as FIFA's being exposed,

655
00:25:01.417 --> 00:25:06.183
just as the NCAA has been exposed, USA gymnastics,

656
00:25:06.183 --> 00:25:09.016
the IOC is being exposed,

657
00:25:09.016 --> 00:25:12.236
and you can just, you know, peel back every layer

658
00:25:12.236 --> 00:25:15.294
of almost every institution and you'll see exposure.

659
00:25:15.294 --> 00:25:19.544
And what is kind of scary for me is when you start,

660
00:25:21.413 --> 00:25:24.359
when all these institutions start to crumble

661
00:25:24.359 --> 00:25:27.548
at different times, what replaces them?

662
00:25:27.548 --> 00:25:30.209
And, you know, so you don't want these things

663
00:25:30.209 --> 00:25:34.045
to completely collapse because we do need institution,

664
00:25:34.045 --> 00:25:37.212
like institutional pillars to keep us,

665
00:25:38.134 --> 00:25:40.446
I think to keep us within the guidelines

666
00:25:40.446 --> 00:25:42.196
of a healthy society.

667
00:25:43.654 --> 00:25:44.862
But at the same time,

668
00:25:44.862 --> 00:25:48.042
you can't abide some of the crimes that we're seeing,

669
00:25:48.042 --> 00:25:49.594
some of the corruption that we're seeing.

670
00:25:49.594 --> 00:25:51.342
So, what happens?

671
00:25:51.342 --> 00:25:52.300
I'm not so sure,

672
00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:56.414
but I agree with you that there's certainly trouble.

673
00:25:56.414 --> 00:26:01.318
I also think though that when fans are back in the arenas

674
00:26:01.318 --> 00:26:03.269
and the Summer Olympics come back around,

675
00:26:03.269 --> 00:26:04.977
which are more popular anyway,

676
00:26:04.977 --> 00:26:07.836
and it's in Paris, it could be a different story.

677
00:26:07.836 --> 00:26:08.669
Yeah, we'll see.

678
00:26:08.669 --> 00:26:10.539
I mean, I guess the question really is,

679
00:26:10.539 --> 00:26:12.078
is the IOC beyond repair?

680
00:26:12.078 --> 00:26:14.767
Can it be, can these problems be resolved

681
00:26:14.767 --> 00:26:18.346
within the current context of how it operates?

682
00:26:18.346 --> 00:26:19.861
Or do we need to, you know,

683
00:26:19.861 --> 00:26:23.462
basically create a new IOC from the ground up?

684
00:26:23.462 --> 00:26:26.025
Like, who polices the IOC?

685
00:26:26.025 --> 00:26:27.763
And how can we root out this corruption

686
00:26:27.763 --> 00:26:30.574
and hold these people accountable and get people in there

687
00:26:30.574 --> 00:26:33.657
who share the values of the Olympics,

688
00:26:34.633 --> 00:26:36.750
you know, that have integrity?

689
00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:37.748
And, you know,

690
00:26:37.748 --> 00:26:40.328
how can we eradicate the undue monetary influences

691
00:26:40.328 --> 00:26:43.660
that are causing some of these, you know, corruptions

692
00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:45.634
and scandals?
I'm not sure you can.

693
00:26:45.634 --> 00:26:46.603
I'm not sure you can.

694
00:26:46.603 --> 00:26:50.496
Because it used to be, it was all about amateur athletics.

695
00:26:50.496 --> 00:26:52.934
That ship sailed a long time ago.

696
00:26:52.934 --> 00:26:53.892
Which is fine.

697
00:26:53.892 --> 00:26:55.100
I mean, I think we wanted,

698
00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.068
we always wanted the best athletes to compete.

699
00:26:57.068 --> 00:26:58.901
So, I mean, it's like,

700
00:27:00.401 --> 00:27:02.636
you need the best in the world to compete.

701
00:27:02.636 --> 00:27:05.764
The athletes need to make a living.

702
00:27:05.764 --> 00:27:08.174
Before you had athletes trying to be amateurs

703
00:27:08.174 --> 00:27:09.335
and they couldn't make a living.

704
00:27:09.335 --> 00:27:12.727
And then afterwards, you know, it's still that way,

705
00:27:12.727 --> 00:27:15.086
but it's at least they have a chance to make a living

706
00:27:15.086 --> 00:27:17.665
when they're at their peak earning potential.

707
00:27:17.665 --> 00:27:19.915
And so, I think it's great.

708
00:27:20.873 --> 00:27:24.693
I think Lindsay Crause wrote a devastating column.

709
00:27:24.693 --> 00:27:25.704
Yeah, she did.

710
00:27:25.704 --> 00:27:27.095
In The New York Times.

711
00:27:27.095 --> 00:27:29.693
And the lead to this story,

712
00:27:29.693 --> 00:27:31.821
I mean, I'll just read a couple paragraphs.

713
00:27:31.821 --> 00:27:34.151
This is what's happening.

714
00:27:34.151 --> 00:27:36.359
This explains why I haven't watched.

715
00:27:36.359 --> 00:27:38.093
"Imagine a dystopian Olympics.

716
00:27:38.093 --> 00:27:40.907
"Maybe it would have athletes skiing on fake snow

717
00:27:40.907 --> 00:27:42.981
"down parched slopes.

718
00:27:42.981 --> 00:27:45.689
"Robots mixing cocktails, making dumplings,

719
00:27:45.689 --> 00:27:46.941
"and disinfecting the air.

720
00:27:46.941 --> 00:27:49.400
"Events staffed by workers, not in sportswear,

721
00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:50.900
"but hazmat suits.

722
00:27:51.847 --> 00:27:52.930
"Instead of a stadium,

723
00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:57.190
"you are eager to get a seat in a bubble you cannot leave."

724
00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:58.023
Yeah.

725
00:27:58.023 --> 00:27:58.981
(Adam laughs)

726
00:27:58.981 --> 00:28:00.189
There's a line in this article,

727
00:28:00.189 --> 00:28:02.953
"The Games wreak of societal decline."

728
00:28:02.953 --> 00:28:03.786
Yes.

729
00:28:03.786 --> 00:28:06.194
It's very, it's a very pessimistic view.

730
00:28:06.194 --> 00:28:10.547
And she says at the bottom of the fourth or fifth paragraph,

731
00:28:10.547 --> 00:28:13.311
"Instead of showcasing the best of what humanity can do,

732
00:28:13.311 --> 00:28:16.980
"this Olympic seems to reflect what we can't."

733
00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:20.514
And so, it's a more pessimistic, you know,

734
00:28:20.514 --> 00:28:23.558
take on kind of what David was talking about in his piece.

735
00:28:23.558 --> 00:28:24.691
It is.

736
00:28:24.691 --> 00:28:25.649
And it's dispiriting,

737
00:28:25.649 --> 00:28:27.254
and it's not helped by the fact

738
00:28:27.254 --> 00:28:28.593
that there's a nuclear power plant

739
00:28:28.593 --> 00:28:30.551
right behind the ski jump.

740
00:28:30.551 --> 00:28:31.509
Yes.

741
00:28:31.509 --> 00:28:32.342
(Rich laughs)

742
00:28:32.342 --> 00:28:33.457
But like...

743
00:28:33.457 --> 00:28:35.040
It's true.
How did that happen?

744
00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:36.258
But then there's a lot of people

745
00:28:36.258 --> 00:28:38.989
who think nuclear power is an answer to the climate crisis,

746
00:28:38.989 --> 00:28:42.663
but the ski jump itself, if you look at it,

747
00:28:42.663 --> 00:28:44.750
if you strip it down, like to people like Eric Heiden,

748
00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:45.664
or the ski jump.

749
00:28:45.664 --> 00:28:47.989
I mean, the ski jump is just mental.

750
00:28:47.989 --> 00:28:50.946
They fly so high and so far.

751
00:28:50.946 --> 00:28:53.160
And I mean, that's one of the great sports,

752
00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.036
that's what the Olympics are about.

753
00:28:55.036 --> 00:28:56.630
These sports you don't normally tune into

754
00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:59.317
that can be so captivating.

755
00:28:59.317 --> 00:29:02.133
So, you know, it still has the core.

756
00:29:02.133 --> 00:29:03.077
The core is still there.

757
00:29:03.077 --> 00:29:05.155
You know, the events, the athletes.

758
00:29:05.155 --> 00:29:06.777
The purity is in the athletes.

759
00:29:06.777 --> 00:29:07.625
It always was, you know.

760
00:29:07.625 --> 00:29:10.103
There's a flickering, tiny little Olympic flame

761
00:29:10.103 --> 00:29:12.076
at the bottom of a very deep,

762
00:29:12.076 --> 00:29:13.060
deep hole.
It's not out yet.

763
00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:16.518
And it still continues to flicker.

764
00:29:16.518 --> 00:29:17.979
Well, let's put a little more water on it,

765
00:29:17.979 --> 00:29:21.967
and then we'll fuel it back up with some good stories.

766
00:29:21.967 --> 00:29:24.055
Yeah, we do have some good stories I want to share.

767
00:29:24.055 --> 00:29:26.207
But what's the final douse of water you want to throw

768
00:29:26.207 --> 00:29:29.312
on this fledgling flame?
The Kamila Valieva

769
00:29:29.312 --> 00:29:30.270
situation.

770
00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:31.103
Yes.

771
00:29:31.103 --> 00:29:32.573
The Kamila Valieva situation.

772
00:29:32.573 --> 00:29:35.156
So, walk me through this.

773
00:29:35.156 --> 00:29:36.985
15 year old Russian figure skater,

774
00:29:36.985 --> 00:29:39.985
basically Nadia Comaneci on the ice,

775
00:29:41.632 --> 00:29:45.632
tested positive for the angina drug in December.

776
00:29:47.772 --> 00:29:51.150
And she basically is this prodigy.

777
00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:54.294
The drug helps you, makes you more efficient, I guess,

778
00:29:54.294 --> 00:29:56.328
in athletic endeavors.

779
00:29:56.328 --> 00:29:58.729
It's called trimeta...

780
00:29:58.729 --> 00:30:00.062
Trimetazidine.

781
00:30:00.062 --> 00:30:03.949
Yeah, it's a cardiac metabolic modulator proven to,

782
00:30:03.949 --> 00:30:07.565
you know, proven to improve physical efficiency.

783
00:30:07.565 --> 00:30:10.689
Anyway, this test came in long before the Olympics,

784
00:30:10.689 --> 00:30:13.769
but it was still tested positive for it.

785
00:30:13.769 --> 00:30:15.477
And it's a 15 year old girl,

786
00:30:15.477 --> 00:30:17.810
so at first they didn't,

787
00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:19.946
the Olympic committee did not identify,

788
00:30:19.946 --> 00:30:22.412
or WADA did not identify who it was.

789
00:30:22.412 --> 00:30:25.499
But she had helped Russia to team gold.

790
00:30:25.499 --> 00:30:29.440
She was favored to win gold in the individual rounds

791
00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:30.523
as well.

792
00:30:30.523 --> 00:30:33.231
And it was sad 'cause you know

793
00:30:33.231 --> 00:30:36.200
if a 15 year old girl is taking a drug like that,

794
00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:38.477
she's doing what she's being asked to do.

795
00:30:38.477 --> 00:30:39.685
Of course.

796
