1087
00:42:12.029 --> 00:42:15.468
So, anyway, I'll link this, this is from Yahoo News,

1088
00:42:15.468 --> 00:42:17.587
I'll link this up in the show notes.

1089
00:42:17.587 --> 00:42:18.545
It's a great story as well.

1090
00:42:18.545 --> 00:42:20.128
It's a pretty cool story.

1091
00:42:20.128 --> 00:42:21.586
The stories are just bonkers.

1092
00:42:21.586 --> 00:42:23.632
I mean, like that's the thing about Olympics,

1093
00:42:23.632 --> 00:42:25.513
and since we're so driven by story,

1094
00:42:25.513 --> 00:42:28.732
I mean, that's why the Olympics, as troubled as they may be,

1095
00:42:28.732 --> 00:42:30.149
will always find,

1096
00:42:31.065 --> 00:42:33.523
they will always be part of the conversation

1097
00:42:33.523 --> 00:42:35.186
'cause these stories are too good.

1098
00:42:35.186 --> 00:42:37.566
And just in case you thought we were done talking

1099
00:42:37.566 --> 00:42:38.680
about speed skating.

1100
00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:39.888
We are not done talking about-

1101
00:42:39.888 --> 00:42:40.935
Not on Rich's watch.

1102
00:42:40.935 --> 00:42:42.234
We are not done talking about speed skating

1103
00:42:42.234 --> 00:42:44.526
'cause the other story that really caught my eye

1104
00:42:44.526 --> 00:42:48.859
that I thought was fascinating is this speed skater,

1105
00:42:49.803 --> 00:42:51.220
Nils van der Poel

1106
00:42:52.269 --> 00:42:55.670
who set the world record in the 10 kilometers.

1107
00:42:55.670 --> 00:43:00.106
And what caught my interest about this was the fact

1108
00:43:00.106 --> 00:43:03.106
that he published a 60 page document

1109
00:43:05.267 --> 00:43:09.254
that chronicles his training philosophy

1110
00:43:09.254 --> 00:43:10.571
and his training logs,

1111
00:43:10.571 --> 00:43:11.823
like everything that went

1112
00:43:11.823 --> 00:43:14.359
into him breaking this world record.

1113
00:43:14.359 --> 00:43:17.609
And you can find that at howtoskate.se.

1114
00:43:18.473 --> 00:43:21.682
And you can download the document and read it yourself.

1115
00:43:21.682 --> 00:43:23.336
Like, here's what the website looks like.

1116
00:43:23.336 --> 00:43:24.919
How to skate a 10K.

1117
00:43:26.272 --> 00:43:28.606
And here's what the document itself looks like.

1118
00:43:28.606 --> 00:43:29.705
Like, it's literally like-

1119
00:43:29.705 --> 00:43:30.538
It's a white paper.

1120
00:43:30.538 --> 00:43:32.681
That's exactly what it is.

1121
00:43:32.681 --> 00:43:34.023
It's a white paper.

1122
00:43:34.023 --> 00:43:38.004
And this started circulating around Twitter.

1123
00:43:38.004 --> 00:43:39.328
Look, you have a Twitter message.

1124
00:43:39.328 --> 00:43:40.161
Oh, I do.

1125
00:43:40.161 --> 00:43:41.119
I wonder if it's from me.

1126
00:43:41.119 --> 00:43:42.202
It might be.

1127
00:43:42.202 --> 00:43:46.788
This started circulating on Twitter and it caught my eye

1128
00:43:46.788 --> 00:43:48.871
by Alan Couzens

1129
00:43:48.871 --> 00:43:53.388
who is one of the world's top endurance coaches,

1130
00:43:53.388 --> 00:43:57.096
also a great follow on Twitter @Alan_Couzens.

1131
00:43:57.096 --> 00:44:00.220
C-O-U-Z-E-N-S.

1132
00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:02.415
That's how I devoured the white paper through this-

1133
00:44:02.415 --> 00:44:03.619
Through his thread.

1134
00:44:03.619 --> 00:44:05.520
So yeah, he published a thread

1135
00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:10.435
that kind of deconstructs this document, this white paper,

1136
00:44:10.435 --> 00:44:13.497
on how to achieve world beating levels of fitness.

1137
00:44:13.497 --> 00:44:15.874
And what it really is and why, you know,

1138
00:44:15.874 --> 00:44:20.116
I felt like it was important to talk about is

1139
00:44:20.116 --> 00:44:22.699
how important this athlete felt

1140
00:44:23.719 --> 00:44:26.466
about building his aerobic base.

1141
00:44:26.466 --> 00:44:28.634
So, it's back to this zone two discussion.

1142
00:44:28.634 --> 00:44:29.467
Yes.

1143
00:44:29.467 --> 00:44:32.189
And how crucial it is to build a massive aerobic base

1144
00:44:32.189 --> 00:44:33.710
that you can then layer

1145
00:44:33.710 --> 00:44:37.775
on top of all of your kind of harder efforts

1146
00:44:37.775 --> 00:44:41.479
to break through the glass ceiling on your performance.

1147
00:44:41.479 --> 00:44:43.812
It's almost like he said,

1148
00:44:43.812 --> 00:44:46.916
what comes out of the white paper is he advocates seasons

1149
00:44:46.916 --> 00:44:48.310
where it's all aerobic base,

1150
00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:52.059
then all kind of interval training and threshold stuff,

1151
00:44:52.059 --> 00:44:54.108
then all aerobic base again.

1152
00:44:54.108 --> 00:44:55.566
Right.

1153
00:44:55.566 --> 00:44:57.193
And he doesn't do any threshold work

1154
00:44:57.193 --> 00:45:01.276
when he was in this like extended period of many months,

1155
00:45:01.276 --> 00:45:02.630
just building this base.

1156
00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:06.630
And when you look at the work that he was doing,

1157
00:45:07.885 --> 00:45:09.936
it's pretty spectacular.

1158
00:45:09.936 --> 00:45:12.033
So, here are just sort of excerpted things

1159
00:45:12.033 --> 00:45:13.715
from his white paper where he is talking

1160
00:45:13.715 --> 00:45:16.268
about the importance of aerobic fitness.

1161
00:45:16.268 --> 00:45:17.985
"The stronger I got aerobically,

1162
00:45:17.985 --> 00:45:20.584
"the more anaerobic sessions I could do later on.

1163
00:45:20.584 --> 00:45:22.167
"I purified the aerobic season."

1164
00:45:22.167 --> 00:45:24.626
Meaning during this number of months,

1165
00:45:24.626 --> 00:45:26.463
he only trained aerobically,

1166
00:45:26.463 --> 00:45:30.546
never went above his zone two level of intensity.

1167
00:45:31.792 --> 00:45:33.322
You think it's zone two only,

1168
00:45:33.322 --> 00:45:35.327
or could it be zone two and zone three?

1169
00:45:35.327 --> 00:45:38.766
I mean, you know, it gets technical with that.

1170
00:45:38.766 --> 00:45:40.856
Like, it's not an on/off switch.

1171
00:45:40.856 --> 00:45:42.934
There's like a range in between those two.

1172
00:45:42.934 --> 00:45:44.142
But I would,

1173
00:45:44.142 --> 00:45:45.557
basically what he's saying is,

1174
00:45:45.557 --> 00:45:48.807
"I am in my aerobic fitness building phase,

1175
00:45:48.807 --> 00:45:51.888
"so I don't exceed that threshold."

1176
00:45:51.888 --> 00:45:55.628
And the top end of his zone two would be max efforts

1177
00:45:55.628 --> 00:45:58.276
for like mortal human beings.

1178
00:45:58.276 --> 00:46:00.137
But he's so fit that he could handle like

1179
00:46:00.137 --> 00:46:02.387
just unbelievable workouts.

1180
00:46:03.765 --> 00:46:06.600
There's another Twitter thread where he,

1181
00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:08.433
here's a typical week.

1182
00:46:08.433 --> 00:46:12.233
Monday, seven hours of biking at 260 Watts.

1183
00:46:12.233 --> 00:46:14.620
So, for people that aren't cyclists,

1184
00:46:14.620 --> 00:46:17.419
maintaining like 260 Watts on average

1185
00:46:17.419 --> 00:46:21.623
over a number of hours is you have to be extremely fit

1186
00:46:21.623 --> 00:46:22.456
to do that.

1187
00:46:22.456 --> 00:46:23.477
He's doing that at zone two.

1188
00:46:23.477 --> 00:46:26.364
Next day, six hours of biking at 250 Watts.

1189
00:46:26.364 --> 00:46:28.845
Wednesday, two hours of cross country skiing,

1190
00:46:28.845 --> 00:46:31.022
four hours of biking at 250 Watts.

1191
00:46:31.022 --> 00:46:34.148
Thursday, seven hours of biking at 265 Watts.

1192
00:46:34.148 --> 00:46:37.130
Friday, six hours of biking at 240 Watts.

1193
00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:38.503
And then Saturday, rest.

1194
00:46:38.503 --> 00:46:39.336
Sunday, rest.

1195
00:46:39.336 --> 00:46:41.357
So, in a five day period,

1196
00:46:41.357 --> 00:46:44.001
he put in 35 hours of aerobic training

1197
00:46:44.001 --> 00:46:45.550
and that was just a typical week.

1198
00:46:45.550 --> 00:46:48.586
So, that gives you an idea of like what a beast this guy is.

1199
00:46:48.586 --> 00:46:49.544
And he was eating like crazy.

1200
00:46:49.544 --> 00:46:52.589
He was having like straight whipped cream.

1201
00:46:52.589 --> 00:46:54.353
7,000 calories a day.

1202
00:46:54.353 --> 00:46:55.186
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1203
00:46:55.186 --> 00:46:56.519
And putting on weight.

1204
00:46:56.519 --> 00:46:58.952
Yeah, he put on five kilos I think.

1205
00:46:58.952 --> 00:47:01.827
Explaining how this is, you know, important.

1206
00:47:01.827 --> 00:47:03.878
Like, a lot of athletes skimp on their calories

1207
00:47:03.878 --> 00:47:05.988
during this phase 'cause they're worried about weight gain,

1208
00:47:05.988 --> 00:47:08.111
and he put on weight and was happy with that.

1209
00:47:08.111 --> 00:47:10.369
And it also is a buffer against, you know,

1210
00:47:10.369 --> 00:47:12.646
injury and over training and these other things.

1211
00:47:12.646 --> 00:47:15.050
So, anyway, fascinating white paper.

1212
00:47:15.050 --> 00:47:17.097
Very good analysis by Alan

1213
00:47:17.097 --> 00:47:20.204
who kind of identifies some core principles

1214
00:47:20.204 --> 00:47:22.236
out of this program,

1215
00:47:22.236 --> 00:47:24.365
which is A, we already mentioned,

1216
00:47:24.365 --> 00:47:27.771
purifying your aerobic season, not having mixed messages,

1217
00:47:27.771 --> 00:47:30.744
the idea that the stronger you get aerobically,

1218
00:47:30.744 --> 00:47:33.240
the more anaerobic sessions you can do later

1219
00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:35.752
because the more aerobically fit you are,

1220
00:47:35.752 --> 00:47:40.404
then you can go anaerobic and go back to base more quickly.

1221
00:47:40.404 --> 00:47:42.036
Like, you bounce back more quickly,

1222
00:47:42.036 --> 00:47:43.226
you recover more quickly.

1223
00:47:43.226 --> 00:47:46.703
So, your capacity for that higher intensity work

1224
00:47:46.703 --> 00:47:50.466
increases the more aerobically fit that you become.

1225
00:47:50.466 --> 00:47:52.022
So, more aerobic capacity,

1226
00:47:52.022 --> 00:47:54.978
faster recovery for hard stuff later.

1227
00:47:54.978 --> 00:47:57.378
And also he talks about finding joy in all of this.

1228
00:47:57.378 --> 00:48:00.047
Like, if you're gonna be putting in that much work,

1229
00:48:00.047 --> 00:48:02.971
how can you make it meaningful and joyous in doing it?

1230
00:48:02.971 --> 00:48:05.253
Otherwise it's not sustainable, right?

1231
00:48:05.253 --> 00:48:08.794
Not everybody is living this guy's monastic lifestyle

1232
00:48:08.794 --> 00:48:11.485
to be an Olympic world champion and world record holder.

1233
00:48:11.485 --> 00:48:13.790
But I think the principles are applicable

1234
00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:15.615
in all of our lives.

1235
00:48:15.615 --> 00:48:17.238
And he even, he ran,

1236
00:48:17.238 --> 00:48:20.075
part of his training was running a 100 mile race.

1237
00:48:20.075 --> 00:48:21.181
Yeah, that's right.

1238
00:48:21.181 --> 00:48:23.062
That's that was part of his aerobic training.

1239
00:48:23.062 --> 00:48:25.771
Which he said was one of the most meaningful

1240
00:48:25.771 --> 00:48:27.254
and impactful things that he did.

1241
00:48:27.254 --> 00:48:30.867
Like, it gave him a new sense of what his capacity was

1242
00:48:30.867 --> 00:48:32.096
and his capability.

1243
00:48:32.096 --> 00:48:33.429
Pretty exciting.

1244
00:48:33.429 --> 00:48:34.762
And this is all for a race.

1245
00:48:34.762 --> 00:48:37.196
Like, 10 kilometers in speed skating,

1246
00:48:37.196 --> 00:48:38.684
how long do you think that takes?

1247
00:48:38.684 --> 00:48:39.517
Right.

1248
00:48:39.517 --> 00:48:40.817
Like, this is-

1249
00:48:40.817 --> 00:48:41.725
What is it?

1250
00:48:41.725 --> 00:48:42.684
It was 12.

1251
00:48:42.684 --> 00:48:44.201
It was 12 and a half, 12:30.

1252
00:48:44.201 --> 00:48:45.347
He set the world record.

1253
00:48:45.347 --> 00:48:47.596
So, all of this aerobic work

1254
00:48:47.596 --> 00:48:50.429
for a 12 and a half minute effort,

1255
00:48:51.406 --> 00:48:55.230
which is you can characterize that as an endurance event.

1256
00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:58.234
Certainly in speed skating, it is an endurance event.

1257
00:48:58.234 --> 00:49:00.423
But still this is not an ultra marathon

1258
00:49:00.423 --> 00:49:01.387
that he's training for.

1259
00:49:01.387 --> 00:49:02.970
It's a 12 minute race.

1260
00:49:02.970 --> 00:49:05.266
And yet he put in that much work

1261
00:49:05.266 --> 00:49:06.481
into building his aerobic base.

1262
00:49:06.481 --> 00:49:08.543
So, I think that's instructive

1263
00:49:08.543 --> 00:49:11.297
for all of the endurance athletes out there.

1264
00:49:11.297 --> 00:49:14.354
I like how that, Alan Couzens gave props

1265
00:49:14.354 --> 00:49:18.049
to the Norwegians saying similar to the Norwegian approach

1266
00:49:18.049 --> 00:49:19.132
in his thread.

1267
00:49:19.132 --> 00:49:20.432
The Norwegian.

1268
00:49:20.432 --> 00:49:23.237
See, I feel like they're more dialed in on quality.

1269
00:49:23.237 --> 00:49:24.445
They do both.

1270
00:49:24.445 --> 00:49:28.112
And they don't purify aerobic and intervals.

1271
00:49:28.947 --> 00:49:32.380
They mix it up, but they are very much about fuel.

1272
00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:34.429
External fuel is the only fuel.

1273
00:49:34.429 --> 00:49:38.769
They're very much about eating and training in particular,

1274
00:49:38.769 --> 00:49:42.860
in a particular way, and staying, you know, true to it.

1275
00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:46.367
They're both Scandinavian, you know, which is interesting.

1276
00:49:46.367 --> 00:49:49.607
Can we talk about my zone two problems?

1277
00:49:49.607 --> 00:49:50.690
Oh, I can't wait.

1278
00:49:50.690 --> 00:49:51.773
Oh, this is good stuff.

1279
00:49:51.773 --> 00:49:52.731
This is really,

1280
00:49:52.731 --> 00:49:54.842
I mean, really this is what the podcast is about.

1281
00:49:54.842 --> 00:49:56.501
Please tell me your zone two problems.

1282
00:49:56.501 --> 00:49:58.359
I'm not sure if you've been keeping track.

1283
00:49:58.359 --> 00:50:01.230
Do you know what your zone two is, Adam?

1284
00:50:01.230 --> 00:50:03.063
Do you know what zone two is?

1285
00:50:03.063 --> 00:50:04.475
You taught me this stuff.

1286
00:50:04.475 --> 00:50:06.892
I think it's like 134 to 147.

1287
00:50:08.212 --> 00:50:09.045
For running.

1288
00:50:09.045 --> 00:50:09.878
For running.

1289
00:50:09.878 --> 00:50:12.670
So, I injured my back at the beginning of the year,

1290
00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:14.767
and I had to take basically a month off.

1291
00:50:14.767 --> 00:50:17.302
I ran twice in four weeks because I couldn't.

1292
00:50:17.302 --> 00:50:18.885
I ran then I got hurt.

1293
00:50:18.885 --> 00:50:20.868
Then I ran once at the two week mark,

1294
00:50:20.868 --> 00:50:22.004
and I just wasn't ready.

1295
00:50:22.004 --> 00:50:23.212
I had to get some PT.

1296
00:50:23.212 --> 00:50:25.910
So, then I came back to it and have been, you know,

1297
00:50:25.910 --> 00:50:28.591
but when I, right before I got hurt, I'd been running.

1298
00:50:28.591 --> 00:50:31.152
My zone two was down to like a 10 minute mile.

1299
00:50:31.152 --> 00:50:35.072
And I started at like 12 plus minute mile for zone two.

1300
00:50:35.072 --> 00:50:37.304
And so I was actually trending really well.

1301
00:50:37.304 --> 00:50:39.818
And then all of a sudden, you know,

1302
00:50:39.818 --> 00:50:40.988
now I'm coming back to it

1303
00:50:40.988 --> 00:50:43.375
and it's almost like starting over.

1304
00:50:43.375 --> 00:50:46.405
Yeah, it will come back more quickly,

1305
00:50:46.405 --> 00:50:49.163
but you can't get around that first phase

1306
00:50:49.163 --> 00:50:51.956
of re-acclimating your body to it.

1307
00:50:51.956 --> 00:50:54.066
So yeah, it won't come back immediately.

1308
00:50:54.066 --> 00:50:55.399
It's gonna take some time,

1309
00:50:55.399 --> 00:50:58.482
but that patience will pay dividends.

1310
00:51:00.183 --> 00:51:01.341
So you'll just have to
Thank you, Rich.

1311
00:51:01.341 --> 00:51:02.196
stick with it.

1312
00:51:02.196 --> 00:51:03.029
I will.

1313
00:51:03.029 --> 00:51:03.987
