that I'm not doing the right thing while I'm quote "young,"

2839
01:51:13.811 --> 01:51:15.394
no matter what it is,

2840
01:51:15.394 --> 01:51:18.467
especially as a person that is pursuing something

2841
01:51:18.467 --> 01:51:20.300
other than college.

2842
01:51:20.300 --> 01:51:22.717
Even though I'm doing the right things to progress

2843
01:51:22.717 --> 01:51:23.550
towards my goals.

2844
01:51:23.550 --> 01:51:25.793
These older people usually make me feel

2845
01:51:25.793 --> 01:51:28.255
like I'm doing the wrong thing or not doing enough.

2846
01:51:28.255 --> 01:51:30.689
Also, how do I enjoy the moment

2847
01:51:30.689 --> 01:51:31.796
while I go through this process

2848
01:51:31.796 --> 01:51:35.070
when I might be doing all this work,

2849
01:51:35.070 --> 01:51:38.496
but not seeing any of the progress in the present time?

2850
01:51:38.496 --> 01:51:40.175
But with that being said, I think it'd be great

2851
01:51:40.175 --> 01:51:43.167
for you both to give an objective statement

2852
01:51:43.167 --> 01:51:45.374
that could apply to all young people,

2853
01:51:45.374 --> 01:51:48.118
so we might have an idea of what the right

2854
01:51:48.118 --> 01:51:49.893
and wrong things are.

2855
01:51:49.893 --> 01:51:51.859
I know this is a pretty dense question,

2856
01:51:51.859 --> 01:51:53.989
so maybe there's a possibility to create

2857
01:51:53.989 --> 01:51:56.759
into a section of one of the wrongs.

2858
01:51:56.759 --> 01:51:58.217
I know it would be very beneficial

2859
01:51:58.217 --> 01:51:59.735
to a lot of people listening.

2860
01:51:59.735 --> 01:52:02.840
Whatever happens, I appreciate you guys taking the time

2861
01:52:02.840 --> 01:52:05.022
for these questions, and I hope you have a great day.

2862
01:52:05.022 --> 01:52:05.855
Thanks.

2863
01:52:06.855 --> 01:52:08.688
Oh, the youth.

2864
01:52:08.688 --> 01:52:09.521
Yes.

2865
01:52:09.521 --> 01:52:10.354
The gen Z.

2866
01:52:10.354 --> 01:52:12.291
As a representative of older people,

2867
01:52:12.291 --> 01:52:15.369
I'd like to hear your take on this.

2868
01:52:15.369 --> 01:52:16.786
The gen X take.

2869
01:52:18.350 --> 01:52:19.433
Yes.

2870
01:52:19.433 --> 01:52:22.676
I think, you know, listen, it is an open ended question.

2871
01:52:22.676 --> 01:52:24.340
It can be interpreted so many different ways.

2872
01:52:24.340 --> 01:52:27.794
But I think it's interesting to get a question like that

2873
01:52:27.794 --> 01:52:29.502
because it shows you,

2874
01:52:29.502 --> 01:52:31.728
'cause often we don't get that.

2875
01:52:31.728 --> 01:52:34.545
You get it at home, but I don't have 20 year olds at home,

2876
01:52:34.545 --> 01:52:39.243
so I don't get that same kind of viewpoint too often.

2877
01:52:39.243 --> 01:52:41.682
So, it's cool to hear it unfiltered here.

2878
01:52:41.682 --> 01:52:43.287
Yeah, I mean, I'm around a lot of people

2879
01:52:43.287 --> 01:52:45.241
that are in Evan's age bracket,

2880
01:52:45.241 --> 01:52:48.179
and, you know, I am aware of how,

2881
01:52:48.179 --> 01:52:53.012
look, there's something like beautiful about young people,

2882
01:52:54.039 --> 01:52:57.157
you know, shirking the trends of their elders

2883
01:52:57.157 --> 01:52:59.921
and, you know, believing that they know what's best,

2884
01:52:59.921 --> 01:53:00.754
you know.

2885
01:53:00.754 --> 01:53:02.931
And as an older person, you're like, there's so much,

2886
01:53:02.931 --> 01:53:05.172
you don't know what you don't know, you know?

2887
01:53:05.172 --> 01:53:06.079
And so, as an older person,

2888
01:53:06.079 --> 01:53:08.482
you always wanna like save people from making mistakes.

2889
01:53:08.482 --> 01:53:12.223
And oftentimes those people need to make those mistakes.

2890
01:53:12.223 --> 01:53:14.593
And then other times they're right.

2891
01:53:14.593 --> 01:53:16.827
And that's what's incredible about young people.

2892
01:53:16.827 --> 01:53:19.853
Like, we can point our finger as old men and be like,

2893
01:53:19.853 --> 01:53:22.955
"These crazy kids," but this is the way of the world.

2894
01:53:22.955 --> 01:53:24.363
Like, the youth inherit the earth

2895
01:53:24.363 --> 01:53:27.849
and they re-shape it in their image.

2896
01:53:27.849 --> 01:53:30.772
And there's something, you know, to be respected about that.

2897
01:53:30.772 --> 01:53:32.530
So, I appreciate Evan's question.

2898
01:53:32.530 --> 01:53:35.199
Yes, it is a bit vague and open ended.

2899
01:53:35.199 --> 01:53:38.366
It's really kind of hard to get a grip

2900
01:53:39.502 --> 01:53:43.114
on what exactly these older people are telling you

2901
01:53:43.114 --> 01:53:44.933
that you're doing that's wrong.

2902
01:53:44.933 --> 01:53:47.665
Like, I don't understand the context

2903
01:53:47.665 --> 01:53:51.568
that's being hurled at you as a specific criticism.

2904
01:53:51.568 --> 01:53:54.142
So, without knowing enough specifics, it's hard to,

2905
01:53:54.142 --> 01:53:56.798
you know, give you a completely concrete answer,

2906
01:53:56.798 --> 01:53:58.465
but I'll do my best.

2907
01:53:59.387 --> 01:54:02.123
And I think the first thing I would say is

2908
01:54:02.123 --> 01:54:05.623
that I feel strongly that youth, your 20s,

2909
01:54:08.449 --> 01:54:11.699
is the time in your life for trying lots of things,

2910
01:54:11.699 --> 01:54:14.598
exposing yourself to as many experiences

2911
01:54:14.598 --> 01:54:19.043
and cultures and jobs and careers as you possibly can

2912
01:54:19.043 --> 01:54:23.023
and being okay with changing your mind, changing your plans,

2913
01:54:23.023 --> 01:54:26.900
changing gears, and being even better with failing.

2914
01:54:26.900 --> 01:54:28.700
This is the time to do it.

2915
01:54:28.700 --> 01:54:31.549
Like, not in your 40s when you have a mortgage and a partner

2916
01:54:31.549 --> 01:54:33.154
and a car payment.

2917
01:54:33.154 --> 01:54:34.612
This is the time.

2918
01:54:34.612 --> 01:54:38.163
And I think that at odds with this and probably

2919
01:54:38.163 --> 01:54:40.804
where some of these older people are coming from

2920
01:54:40.804 --> 01:54:43.387
is this culture that we live in

2921
01:54:44.396 --> 01:54:47.602
that reinforces this mentality that we're in a race.

2922
01:54:47.602 --> 01:54:50.014
And if you're not on a certain pre-approved track,

2923
01:54:50.014 --> 01:54:52.293
that you're falling behind.

2924
01:54:52.293 --> 01:54:55.154
But falling behind what exactly?

2925
01:54:55.154 --> 01:54:58.696
So much of this is like illusion and projection.

2926
01:54:58.696 --> 01:55:01.863
But that projection or that fear comes

2927
01:55:03.123 --> 01:55:05.087
from a very specific place.

2928
01:55:05.087 --> 01:55:09.009
Because as a parent, I can sympathize with their concern

2929
01:55:09.009 --> 01:55:11.723
because we all want our kids to be happy, productive,

2930
01:55:11.723 --> 01:55:13.541
safe, secure.

2931
01:55:13.541 --> 01:55:15.272
We only know what we know,

2932
01:55:15.272 --> 01:55:17.414
and what we know is how we did it

2933
01:55:17.414 --> 01:55:18.772
and how our parents did it.

2934
01:55:18.772 --> 01:55:21.939
And breaking free of social paradigms,

2935
01:55:22.812 --> 01:55:24.439
like when you see a young person who's like,

2936
01:55:24.439 --> 01:55:25.694
"I'm not gonna follow that program.

2937
01:55:25.694 --> 01:55:28.136
"I'm gonna do it my own way,"

2938
01:55:28.136 --> 01:55:31.184
can be scary for an older person or a parent

2939
01:55:31.184 --> 01:55:33.408
because it's not only a threat

2940
01:55:33.408 --> 01:55:35.561
to kind of sometimes our values

2941
01:55:35.561 --> 01:55:38.622
or the way we made our way in the world

2942
01:55:38.622 --> 01:55:40.602
and the way we'd like to see our children make it,

2943
01:55:40.602 --> 01:55:42.621
like we want to pass down our wisdom,

2944
01:55:42.621 --> 01:55:44.596
but also a threat to that compulsion

2945
01:55:44.596 --> 01:55:48.315
that we have as parents for control and for certainty.

2946
01:55:48.315 --> 01:55:51.794
But like I said, those impulses are quite often

2947
01:55:51.794 --> 01:55:53.382
generally wrongheaded

2948
01:55:53.382 --> 01:55:55.672
and they're based on this kind of illusion

2949
01:55:55.672 --> 01:55:56.880
of control anyway.

2950
01:55:56.880 --> 01:55:59.213
So, what I say to you, Evan,

2951
01:56:00.203 --> 01:56:02.251
is that you should follow your heart,

2952
01:56:02.251 --> 01:56:04.022
you should pursue what gets you excited,

2953
01:56:04.022 --> 01:56:07.292
what gets you out bed in the morning.

2954
01:56:07.292 --> 01:56:08.750
I would suggest

2955
01:56:08.750 --> 01:56:12.632
that you might enjoy reading David Epstein's book "Range."

2956
01:56:12.632 --> 01:56:14.342
David who came up earlier on the podcast.

2957
01:56:14.342 --> 01:56:15.675
Got a double mention.

2958
01:56:15.675 --> 01:56:16.508
I know.

2959
01:56:16.508 --> 01:56:17.591
He's getting a double mention today.

2960
01:56:17.591 --> 01:56:18.924
What's up, David?

2961
01:56:18.924 --> 01:56:20.507
Where's that check?

2962
01:56:21.632 --> 01:56:23.914
And in that book, it's sort of a canvasing

2963
01:56:23.914 --> 01:56:26.446
of all these amazingly successful people

2964
01:56:26.446 --> 01:56:28.500
across a wide variety of fields.

2965
01:56:28.500 --> 01:56:32.740
And David realized that kind of what unites these people

2966
01:56:32.740 --> 01:56:35.626
or the trends that he sees in the arc of their life is

2967
01:56:35.626 --> 01:56:37.876
that these people were dabblers.

2968
01:56:37.876 --> 01:56:39.209
They tried many, many things

2969
01:56:39.209 --> 01:56:40.920
before they finally settled on the one thing

2970
01:56:40.920 --> 01:56:42.728
that they found purpose in.

2971
01:56:42.728 --> 01:56:45.457
And all of those dabbling endeavors that they did

2972
01:56:45.457 --> 01:56:47.581
over the course of their life kind of come into play

2973
01:56:47.581 --> 01:56:52.498
to create this stew that makes them kind of the only person

2974
01:56:53.883 --> 01:56:56.090
who could do the thing that they end up doing

2975
01:56:56.090 --> 01:56:58.360
'cause it's this mix of interesting past experiences

2976
01:56:58.360 --> 01:56:59.438
and skillsets.

2977
01:56:59.438 --> 01:57:01.675
But I say this with two caveats,

2978
01:57:01.675 --> 01:57:05.393
and the first is that, you know, following your heart,

2979
01:57:05.393 --> 01:57:08.010
pursuing what gets you excited is not an excuse

2980
01:57:08.010 --> 01:57:09.677
to be irresponsible.

2981
01:57:11.513 --> 01:57:13.663
You still have to live in the world.

2982
01:57:13.663 --> 01:57:16.445
And if you're gonna do this, you have to live lean,

2983
01:57:16.445 --> 01:57:19.540
so you can invest whatever money and time you have

2984
01:57:19.540 --> 01:57:23.093
in experiences and not get captured by debt or obligations

2985
01:57:23.093 --> 01:57:24.312
that don't serve you.

2986
01:57:24.312 --> 01:57:25.615
So, that's super important.

2987
01:57:25.615 --> 01:57:27.311
Don't end up leveraging yourself,

2988
01:57:27.311 --> 01:57:30.990
so that you owe a bunch of people or you have payments

2989
01:57:30.990 --> 01:57:33.861
or you're in an apartment that's too expensive for you.

2990
01:57:33.861 --> 01:57:34.694
All of those things.

2991
01:57:34.694 --> 01:57:36.877
Like, try to live as leanly as possible.

2992
01:57:36.877 --> 01:57:39.257
And that just provides more opportunities

2993
01:57:39.257 --> 01:57:42.324
and also serves your ability to like change gears

2994
01:57:42.324 --> 01:57:43.657
if you want to.

2995
01:57:43.657 --> 01:57:47.116
And the second thing I would say,

2996
01:57:47.116 --> 01:57:49.151
and I say this because I have no idea if you should

2997
01:57:49.151 --> 01:57:50.973
or should not go to college or should

2998
01:57:50.973 --> 01:57:53.265
or should not be doing what you're doing right now,

2999
01:57:53.265 --> 01:57:57.782
is to do a really honest and open inventory of yourself

3000
01:57:57.782 --> 01:57:59.326
and your actions

3001
01:57:59.326 --> 01:58:03.576
because perhaps just maybe your elders are correct,

3002
01:58:04.435 --> 01:58:08.389
and you're not seeing something that they can see.

3003
01:58:08.389 --> 01:58:11.135
And because you're young

3004
01:58:11.135 --> 01:58:13.887
and, you know, full of vim and vigor,

3005
01:58:13.887 --> 01:58:16.164
you have your blinders up to, you know,

3006
01:58:16.164 --> 01:58:17.857
something they're trying to express to you

3007
01:58:17.857 --> 01:58:20.389
that you've just sort of willingly

3008
01:58:20.389 --> 01:58:22.521
or unwillingly blinded yourself to.

3009
01:58:22.521 --> 01:58:23.604
So, in that inventory,

3010
01:58:23.604 --> 01:58:27.011
it's about doing a really honest accounting of your actions

3011
01:58:27.011 --> 01:58:29.872
to identify possible blind spots.

3012
01:58:29.872 --> 01:58:32.404
Like, are there areas in your life

3013
01:58:32.404 --> 01:58:34.423
where maybe you are being irresponsible

3014
01:58:34.423 --> 01:58:39.005
or reactive or petulant, and what are your true motivations

3015
01:58:39.005 --> 01:58:40.955
for what you're pursuing right now?

3016
01:58:40.955 --> 01:58:43.861
Are they truly born out of like a passion

3017
01:58:43.861 --> 01:58:46.615
and an intuitive knowing pull

3018
01:58:46.615 --> 01:58:49.302
towards something personally meaningful

3019
01:58:49.302 --> 01:58:51.027
that is girded with purpose?

3020
01:58:51.027 --> 01:58:54.776
Or is there a little bit of like fuck you to your parents

3021
01:58:54.776 --> 01:58:55.859
built in there somewhere

3022
01:58:55.859 --> 01:58:57.682
because that's true of a lot of young people,

3023
01:58:57.682 --> 01:58:59.387
maybe most young people.

3024
01:58:59.387 --> 01:59:01.983
Like, to what extent are you being driven,

3025
01:59:01.983 --> 01:59:04.854
not by this honorable, you know, sentiment,

3026
01:59:04.854 --> 01:59:07.693
but by some kind of latent resentment

3027
01:59:07.693 --> 01:59:10.705
or other kind of petty emotion that could trip you up

3028
01:59:10.705 --> 01:59:15.622
and, you know, cause a lot of undue suffering in your life.

3029
01:59:16.473 --> 01:59:18.803
So, I think getting that part right is

3030
01:59:18.803 --> 01:59:20.716
absolutely fundamental and key.

3031
01:59:20.716 --> 01:59:21.799
Getting clear on the motivation.

3032
01:59:21.799 --> 01:59:23.549
Yeah, like being really clear

3033
01:59:23.549 --> 01:59:25.684
with what it is you're exactly doing

3034
01:59:25.684 --> 01:59:27.868
and what your motivations are.

3035
01:59:27.868 --> 01:59:29.751
And the only way to get that clarity is

3036
01:59:29.751 --> 01:59:31.834
to really do this inside work.

3037
01:59:31.834 --> 01:59:33.377
And everyone's like, "Well, what is the inside work?"

3038
01:59:33.377 --> 01:59:38.294
And it's hard to give a concrete, clear definition of that,

3039
01:59:39.799 --> 01:59:44.322
but, you know, it's something that you discover

3040
01:59:44.322 --> 01:59:47.584
through kind of grappling with your interior emotions.

3041
01:59:47.584 --> 01:59:49.671
And that can be done with a therapist,

3042
01:59:49.671 --> 01:59:51.338
it can be done through journaling,

3043
01:59:51.338 --> 01:59:53.239
it can be done through mindfulness practices.

3044
01:59:53.239 --> 01:59:56.733
But I really think talking these things out with somebody

3045
01:59:56.733 --> 02:00:00.620
who is skilled at helping you kind of get this type

3046
02:00:00.620 --> 02:00:02.006
of clarity is really important.

3047
02:00:02.006 --> 02:00:05.453
So, if you're capable of seeing a therapist

3048
02:00:05.453 --> 02:00:07.761
or being in some kind of therapeutic construct,

3049
02:00:07.761 --> 02:00:10.753
I think that would be helpful in trying to cobble together

3050
02:00:10.753 --> 02:00:13.670
that kind of inventory and clarity.

3051
02:00:15.461 --> 02:00:17.885
The other piece that he mentioned was progress,

3052
02:00:17.885 --> 02:00:21.582
like not feeling like he was making any progress.

3053
02:00:21.582 --> 02:00:26.432
And to that I would say that nothing in life is for not,

3054
02:00:26.432 --> 02:00:28.544
everything good takes a lot longer

3055
02:00:28.544 --> 02:00:29.752
than you can possibly imagine.

3056
02:00:29.752 --> 02:00:31.210
I'm an example of that.

3057
02:00:31.210 --> 02:00:33.197
And if you feel like you're not making,

3058
02:00:33.197 --> 02:00:34.530
Adam raised his hand.

3059
02:00:34.530 --> 02:00:37.209
If you're not making progress under your definition

3060
02:00:37.209 --> 02:00:41.554
of what progress is, perhaps you could change,

3061
02:00:41.554 --> 02:00:43.898
you might want to change the definition of progress

3062
02:00:43.898 --> 02:00:46.841
or change or extend the timeline,

3063
02:00:46.841 --> 02:00:47.924
and ask yourself,

3064
02:00:47.924 --> 02:00:51.512
irrespective of all of that progress regression,

3065
02:00:51.512 --> 02:00:53.651
like what are you learning along the way

3066
02:00:53.651 --> 02:00:56.498
that could be helpful to you in other facets of life?

3067
02:00:56.498 --> 02:00:58.920
Because even if you feel like you're not making progress,

3068
02:00:58.920 --> 02:01:01.408
if you're grappling with whatever problems you're trying

3069
02:01:01.408 --> 02:01:02.781
to solve or business you're trying to build

3070
02:01:02.781 --> 02:01:05.779
or whatever it is, everything that you're experiencing,

3071
02:01:05.779 --> 02:01:08.170
particularly the setbacks and the failures

3072
02:01:08.170 --> 02:01:11.348
and how you kind of tackle and overcome obstacles,

3073
02:01:11.348 --> 02:01:16.348
that's the curriculum for whatever you end up doing later.

3074
02:01:16.760 --> 02:01:18.218
Even if you never make progress

3075
02:01:18.218 --> 02:01:19.996
in let's say this business venture

3076
02:01:19.996 --> 02:01:22.863
that you're, you know, striving towards building,

3077
02:01:22.863 --> 02:01:26.896
you are learning many important life lessons along the way

3078
02:01:26.896 --> 02:01:30.237
that I have no doubt will come into play in a positive

3079
02:01:30.237 --> 02:01:33.321
and material way later in your life.

3080
02:01:33.321 --> 02:01:36.071
So, I guess that's my big speech.

3081
02:01:37.279 --> 02:01:38.112
Be nimble.

3082
02:01:40.237 --> 02:01:41.542
You know, if you really feel

3083
02:01:41.542 --> 02:01:43.000
like you're not making progress,

3084
02:01:43.000 --> 02:01:45.876
you can't be afraid to, you can't hold on so tightly.

3085
02:01:45.876 --> 02:01:48.101
You should be able to let go.

3086
02:01:48.101 --> 02:01:51.338
And if it's not working, change gears, switch it up.

3087
02:01:51.338 --> 02:01:53.559
This is what your 20s is for.

3088
02:01:53.559 --> 02:01:55.100
You know, the youth is built for this.

3089
02:01:55.100 --> 02:01:59.515
And if you are living lean and ensuring that you're not

3090
02:01:59.515 --> 02:02:02.041
under any kind of undue financial constraints,

3091
02:02:02.041 --> 02:02:05.380
then you're able to do that, and you can pivot your energy

3092
02:02:05.380 --> 02:02:07.885
and just do something else and not beat yourself up

3093
02:02:07.885 --> 02:02:10.289
as if you had failed at something

3094
02:02:10.289 --> 02:02:12.625
despite whatever these elders are telling you

3095
02:02:12.625 --> 02:02:15.394
and kind of, you know, apply the lessons that you learned

3096
02:02:15.394 --> 02:02:18.644
in that past experience into a new one.

3097
02:02:19.540 --> 02:02:21.303
Well said.

3098
02:02:21.303 --> 02:02:24.580
It's the exact speech I give to the scoopers on the line

3099
02:02:24.580 --> 02:02:25.413
every morning.

3100
02:02:25.413 --> 02:02:26.379
The scoopers on the line?

3101
02:02:26.379 --> 02:02:27.212
Yeah.

3102
02:02:27.212 --> 02:02:28.378
What is a scooper and what line?

3103
02:02:28.378 --> 02:02:29.336
Ben and Jerry's.

3104
02:02:29.336 --> 02:02:30.544
Oh.

3105
02:02:30.544 --> 02:02:33.434
(everyone laughs)

3106
02:02:33.434 --> 02:02:34.642
The young scoopers.

3107
02:02:34.642 --> 02:02:37.569
I forgot that you have a day job.

3108
02:02:37.569 --> 02:02:38.402
Oh.

3109
02:02:39.402 --> 02:02:42.111
Just so we're clear, this is a joke, everybody.

3110
02:02:42.111 --> 02:02:43.719
Like, some people actually think you work

3111
02:02:43.719 --> 02:02:44.687
at Ben and Jerry's.

3112
02:02:44.687 --> 02:02:48.294
I don't work at Ben and Jerry's-ish.

3113
02:02:48.294 --> 02:02:49.537
I think it's a great question.

3114
02:02:49.537 --> 02:02:50.495
I think you handled it.

3115
02:02:50.495 --> 02:02:51.328
I think-

3116
02:02:51.328 --> 02:02:52.161
I mean, the question's pretty vague.

3117
02:02:52.161 --> 02:02:53.644
Like, I can't really tell exactly

3118
02:02:53.644 --> 02:02:55.267
what his elders are upset about or what-

3119
02:02:55.267 --> 02:02:56.121
But you can tell

3120
02:02:56.121 --> 02:02:57.204
that there's some sort

3121
02:02:57.204 --> 02:02:58.709
of generational pressure he's getting.

3122
02:02:58.709 --> 02:03:01.454
He's getting pressure from his parents or some other people

3123
02:03:01.454 --> 02:03:02.412
that are like, you know-

3124
02:03:02.412 --> 02:03:03.745
"You should go to college.

3125
02:03:03.745 --> 02:03:04.703
"This is crazy."

3126
02:03:04.703 --> 02:03:06.504
"What you're doing isn't gonna work."

3127
02:03:06.504 --> 02:03:09.135
All I can say is what I was doing

3128
02:03:09.135 --> 02:03:11.103
certainly didn't seem like it was working

3129
02:03:11.103 --> 02:03:12.379
for a long, long, long time.

3130
02:03:12.379 --> 02:03:17.023
And so, you know, but at the same time, I got to a point

3131
02:03:17.023 --> 02:03:18.275
where there was no turning back.

3132
02:03:18.275 --> 02:03:21.787
So, there is something to the commitment of it.

3133
02:03:21.787 --> 02:03:23.815
But core to that in your example.

3134
02:03:23.815 --> 02:03:26.447
There was some knowing that you were a writer.

3135
02:03:26.447 --> 02:03:28.192
Like, "I am a writer."

3136
02:03:28.192 --> 02:03:29.764
What that looks like in the world

3137
02:03:29.764 --> 02:03:31.471
and how I make a living doing that

3138
02:03:31.471 --> 02:03:34.295
that's where you had to be flexible and nimble,

3139
02:03:34.295 --> 02:03:36.824
and there was a lot of different incarnations of that

3140
02:03:36.824 --> 02:03:37.920
along the way.
There was.

3141
02:03:37.920 --> 02:03:38.850
I was a writer.

3142
02:03:38.850 --> 02:03:41.305
But you were always a writer and you knew that.

3143
02:03:41.305 --> 02:03:43.446
So, you had that self-awareness and self knowledge.

3144
02:03:43.446 --> 02:03:46.182
You know, maybe Evan believes in his heart of hearts,

3145
02:03:46.182 --> 02:03:47.732
he is an entrepreneur, he is a business person.

3146
02:03:47.732 --> 02:03:49.406
It's unclear.

3147
02:03:49.406 --> 02:03:52.550
But I think that's where it all starts, right?

3148
02:03:52.550 --> 02:03:54.627
And if you don't know that,

3149
02:03:54.627 --> 02:03:59.413
all the more reason to do, as David Epstein would suggest,

3150
02:03:59.413 --> 02:04:03.087
dabble, you know, spend your 20s exposing yourself

3151
02:04:03.087 --> 02:04:04.606
to lots and lots of things

3152
02:04:04.606 --> 02:04:06.439
'cause how are you supposed to know?

3153
02:04:06.439 --> 02:04:08.926
I do think there is this enormous pressure on young people

3154
02:04:08.926 --> 02:04:11.211
that you're supposed to know what you want to do

3155
02:04:11.211 --> 02:04:12.044
with your life.

3156
02:04:12.044 --> 02:04:13.252
I mean, you go to college at 18,

3157
02:04:13.252 --> 02:04:14.462
and you have to choose a major.

3158
02:04:14.462 --> 02:04:15.295
It's insane.

3159
02:04:15.295 --> 02:04:16.753
That's what I was about to say is like,

3160
02:04:16.753 --> 02:04:19.159
as much as fun as I had college and meeting my friends

3161
02:04:19.159 --> 02:04:19.992
and all that,

3162
02:04:19.992 --> 02:04:22.721
in all honesty, I shouldn't have gone for two years.

3163
02:04:22.721 --> 02:04:25.867
Like, because I didn't take anything specific

3164
02:04:25.867 --> 02:04:28.540
to something I wanna do later, which is fine too.

3165
02:04:28.540 --> 02:04:30.058
Mostly the best thing about college

3166
02:04:30.058 --> 02:04:32.514
for me was meeting friends and reading some books

3167
02:04:32.514 --> 02:04:34.989
that helped me down the line and at the time.

3168
02:04:34.989 --> 02:04:36.381
But like the bottom line is

3169
02:04:36.381 --> 02:04:38.746
if you do ever decide to go back,

3170
02:04:38.746 --> 02:04:40.412
you're gonna know what you want to go back for,

3171
02:04:40.412 --> 02:04:42.119
and you're gonna get more out of the experience,

3172
02:04:42.119 --> 02:04:44.259
and you'll probably pay more attention.

3173
02:04:44.259 --> 02:04:46.092
So, I don't think there's any big problem

3174
02:04:46.092 --> 02:04:47.675
with punting college at all.

3175
02:04:47.675 --> 02:04:48.883
And I'm with you.

3176
02:04:48.883 --> 02:04:51.716
I think that this program that people are on is a bit crazy.

3177
02:04:51.716 --> 02:04:53.456
Yeah, and I would say that, you know,

3178
02:04:53.456 --> 02:04:56.374
my history demonstrates, you know,

3179
02:04:56.374 --> 02:04:58.905
me being a terrible example of all the advice

3180
02:04:58.905 --> 02:04:59.988
that I just gave.

3181
02:04:59.988 --> 02:05:01.222
Like, I went to law school.

3182
02:05:01.222 --> 02:05:02.564
I didn't know why I was going to law school.

3183
02:05:02.564 --> 02:05:05.752
I ended up practicing law for like a really long time.

3184
02:05:05.752 --> 02:05:08.594
Never enjoying it, never having any passion for it.

3185
02:05:08.594 --> 02:05:11.896
But so wed to the idea that this is what I had to do

3186
02:05:11.896 --> 02:05:14.693
because I had invested so much into it.

3187
02:05:14.693 --> 02:05:17.177
And I had put myself in a position

3188
02:05:17.177 --> 02:05:18.836
where I couldn't be nimble,

3189
02:05:18.836 --> 02:05:21.612
and I was overspending and created, you know,

3190
02:05:21.612 --> 02:05:24.065
financial instability that made it even worse.

3191
02:05:24.065 --> 02:05:26.398
It was like terrible.

3192
02:05:26.398 --> 02:05:27.616
And I'm just like that-

3193
02:05:27.616 --> 02:05:28.750
Even that won't work out.

3194
02:05:28.750 --> 02:05:30.567
But that's why I feel so strongly about this.

3195
02:05:30.567 --> 02:05:32.386
Like, please don't do what I did.

3196
02:05:32.386 --> 02:05:33.795
You know, please don't do it.

3197
02:05:33.795 --> 02:05:35.753
And that's probably all your,

3198
02:05:35.753 --> 02:05:37.767
the older generation is trying to tell you

3199
02:05:37.767 --> 02:05:40.875
is please don't fuck up like we fucked up.

3200
02:05:40.875 --> 02:05:42.959
But at the same time, the fear would be like,

3201
02:05:42.959 --> 02:05:44.959
oh, go to law school, it's secure.

3202
02:05:44.959 --> 02:05:49.309
And yeah, if you lease a BMW and live in a nice condo

3203
02:05:49.309 --> 02:05:50.634
and have a prestigious job,

3204
02:05:50.634 --> 02:05:52.094
then I can go to the cocktail party

3205
02:05:52.094 --> 02:05:54.523
and tell my friends that you're doing awesome.

3206
02:05:54.523 --> 02:05:56.273
And that's fucked up.

3207
02:05:57.356 --> 02:05:58.496
And also, unless, we don't know,

3208
02:05:58.496 --> 02:06:00.744
Evan could be a young Walter White in training.

3209
02:06:00.744 --> 02:06:02.723
We just don't know what his business is.

3210
02:06:02.723 --> 02:06:05.454
Do you think he purposely was vague

3211
02:06:05.454 --> 02:06:07.912
to not incriminate himself?

3212
02:06:07.912 --> 02:06:09.995
Walter White shout out.

3213
02:06:11.495 --> 02:06:12.925
That's just a joke, Evan.

3214
02:06:12.925 --> 02:06:15.494
I hope you're doing good in Iowa.

3215
